.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt SCHED 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sched
.Nd thread scheduling policies and processor shares
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Scheduling policy, priority, nice value, and affinity belong to a thread.
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2
changes policy and priority,
.Xr sched_setattr 2
also sets an ordinary thread's slice, and
.Xr setpriority 2
changes its nice value.
.Ss Ordinary policies
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH ,
and
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
are the fair class, all with static scheduling priority zero.
Each processor has its own fair queue.
The scheduler charges a running thread for processor time, excluding
interrupt service and steal time reported by the hypervisor.
Virtual runtime advances by the charged time scaled inversely to the
thread's weight.
A thread with a lower nice value has more weight.
For example, nice 0, 5, and 19 have weights 1024, 335, and 15.
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
has weight 3 regardless of its nice value.
.Pp
The fair class uses earliest eligible virtual deadline first (EEVDF),
as in Linux 6.12.
A thread is eligible when its virtual runtime is at or below the
queue's weighted average.
Among eligible threads the scheduler chooses by earliest virtual
deadline, subject to the running thread's slice protection.
A shorter slice gives an earlier deadline at the same virtual runtime
and weight.
If an ineligible thread sleeps, its dequeue can be delayed so that the
other threads can catch up to its virtual runtime.
The sleeping thread cannot be selected to run.
.Pp
A waking
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
thread can preempt a fair thread if selection favors it.
A wake of a thread outside
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
can preempt a thread of that policy.
Otherwise wakes of
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
and
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
do not preempt the running fair thread.
.Pp
The default base slice at boot is 750,000 ns multiplied by one plus the
integer base-2 logarithm of the online processor count capped at eight.
Linux 6.12 starts from 700,000 ns with the same default scaling.
.Xr debugfs 5
describes the file that reads and sets the base slice.
.Pp
Wake placement and balancing honor the thread's affinity and use
processor load and topology.
The fair queues balance at periodic ticks and on a processor about to
go idle.
A processor whose idle tick has stopped can be kicked to balance.
.Ss Real-time policies
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR
have static priorities from 1 to 99, highest first.
A real-time thread normally runs ahead of fair threads.
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
keeps the processor against threads of equal priority until it blocks
or yields.
.Dv SCHED_RR
also rotates behind its peers when its slice expires, 100 ms by default.
Preemption by a higher priority leaves the remaining slice and place
among equal priorities intact.
A priority-inheriting futex waiter can temporarily give its effective
priority to the owner.
.Pp
A fair server on each processor reserves 50 ms of every 1,000 ms by
default for ordinary work, as on Debian's Linux 6.12 kernel.
Time the ordinary threads already ran counts toward the budget.
When the remaining time in the period is needed to meet the budget,
the fair server allows ordinary work to run ahead of real-time work.
Rach2 gives a futex owner whose priority was raised by a real-time
donation precedence over the fair server.
Linux runs the fair server in the deadline class, above real-time
priorities.
The real-time bandwidth sysctls do not throttle real-time threads on
either system.
.Pp
Linux's
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
class schedules admitted threads by deadline and limits them to their
runtime in each period.
Rach2 checks the arguments, privilege, and affinity, and then refuses
admission with
.Er EBUSY .
.Dv SCHED_EXT
is also unavailable on Rach2.
.Ss Autogroup
Autogroup scheduling is enabled by default.
.Xr setsid 2
creates an autogroup for the caller's new session, and children inherit
their parent's group.
On a processor, runnable autogroups compete by group weight.
Within a group, runnable ordinary threads compete by thread weight.
Two equally weighted autogroups pinned to one processor get comparable
shares under sustained contention, even if one has more runnable
threads.
Real-time scheduling is independent of autogroup membership.
.Pp
The
.Pa /proc/PID/autogroup
file sets the group's nice value.
Disabling
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/sched_autogroup_enabled
makes threads compete without the session weights.
Their group assignments persist and are used again when the switch is
enabled.
Rach2 has no CPU cgroup controller; on Linux a CPU cgroup other than
the root group takes precedence over autogroup.
